always 5. 15-6-256
always 5\x06D4 15-6-256
always 5\x060C 15-6-2
always 5, 15-6-2
always 5\x061F 15-6-236
always 5? 15-6-236
always 5! 15-6-235
always 5\x061B 15-6-25
always 5; 15-6-25
always 6. 124-6-256
always 6\x06D4 124-6-256
always 6\x060C 124-6-2
always 6, 124-6-2
always 6\x061F 124-6-236
always 6? 124-6-236
always 6! 124-6-235
always 6\x061B 124-6-25
always 6; 124-6-25
always 7. 1245-6-256
always 7\x06D4 1245-6-256
always 7\x060C 1245-6-2
always 7, 1245-6-2
always 7\x061F 1245-6-236
always 7? 1245-6-236
always 7! 1245-6-235
always 7\x061B 1245-6-25
always 7; 1245-6-25
always 8. 125-6-256
always 8\x06D4 125-6-256
always 8\x060C 125-6-2
always 8, 125-6-2
always 8\x061F 125-6-236
always 8? 125-6-236
always 8! 125-6-235
always 8\x061B 125-6-25
always 8; 125-6-25
always 9. 24-6-256
always 9\x06D4 24-6-256
always 9\x060C 24-6-2
always 9, 24-6-2
always 9\x061F 24-6-236
always 9? 24-6-236
always 9! 24-6-235
always 9\x061B 24-6-25
always 9; 24-6-25

# Back-translation only: a dot 6 immediately after a digit is the numeric
# terminator of the guide (p.43), not a shad.  Remove it before pass 1.
nofor pass2 @1-6 @1
nofor pass2 @12-6 @12
nofor pass2 @14-6 @14
nofor pass2 @145-6 @145
nofor pass2 @15-6 @15
nofor pass2 @124-6 @124
nofor pass2 @1245-6 @1245
nofor pass2 @125-6 @125
nofor pass2 @24-6 @24
nofor pass2 @245-6 @245

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATUS
#
# FORWARD (Sindhi -> braille) is complete for prose and cross-validated three
# ways: against an independent implementation written from the same sources
# (`sindhi_braille.py`), against Riaz Hussain Memon reading embossed output by
# touch, and against 74 worked examples printed in the Authority's own guide,
# which it reproduces cell for cell.
#
# BACKWARD (braille -> Sindhi) is correct for ordinary prose.  Two limits:
#
#   dots 3456-145-6-256      -> "4" + ڍ   (should be "4.")
#
# liblouis's numeric mode takes precedence over the table's own rules once a
# number sign is present, so trailing punctuation is read as the letter that
# shares its cell.  The pass2 rules below remove the spurious shad, which is as
# far as the table can go without a change in liblouis itself.
#
#   dots 236 at the end of a word -> ھ, never ؟
#
# ھ and the question mark share the cell.  Sindhi forms aspirates by writing ھ
# after a consonant and a great many ordinary words end that way, so the letter
# is the commoner reading and the one this table gives.  The reference
# implementation uses a Sindhi word list and recovers both.
#
# For authoritative back-translation use `sindhi_braille.py`.  Measured on
# 23,434 word tokens from the committee's own publications it round-trips at
# 99.94%; the residue is the ع abbreviation, which needs sentence context.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN THIS TABLE
#
# The code contains one rule liblouis cannot state: a word repeated twice is
# written once followed by dot 3 twice (آهستي آهستي -> آهستي + 3 3).  Deciding
# that requires comparing one word with the previous word, which the rule
# language cannot test.  It is handled outside the table; see the reference
# implementation `sindhi_braille.py` in the project repository.
#
# FRACTIONS.  Guide pp.52-53: the numerator is written in the ordinary digits
# and the denominator in the same digits dropped to the lower dots (1->2, 2->23,
# 3->25, 4->256, 5->26, 6->235, 7->2356, 8->236, 9->35, 0->356), with no bar and
# with the numerator omitted entirely when it is 1.  Deciding that requires
# looking at both sides of the slash, which the rule language cannot do; it is
# handled in `sindhi_braille.py`.
#
# ORDINALS, the omission sign, the poetry and pen-name marks, the footnote star
# and the foreign-language marks are likewise outside the rule language, for the
# same reason: each needs to see more than one word at a time.
#
# Grade 2 contractions are not in this table.  They are in sd-pk-g2.ctb, which
# includes this one and adds 237 contractions in six prefix series - (none), 5,
# 45, 456, 3456 and 6 - plus the six ڳانڊڙا groups written inside a word.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
